it's nearly five weeks since rebecca  lucas submitted a visa application to  bring illona and her daughter marta to  westcliff on sea in essex and away from  the terror in their home town of turner  pill they they had to escape quickly so  they have to escape really quickly  elona's husband stanislav wrote a note  giving his consent for his daughter to  travel to the uk with his wife while he  stayed to fight  but still they wait i don't want to be  lied to i'm fed up of being lied to  um  i want i want the truth you know if my  doctor if you know if their documents  have been lost  i want to know that they've been lost  and how have they been lost their case  is far from unique in theory  applications are dealt with  chronologically but barrister amanda  jones isn't convinced she's taken on  nine judicial review cases of ukrainian  refugees experiencing similar weights  the initial form was extraordinarily  cumbersome and repetitive  and required a separate form for each  member of the family including babies  it's been streamlined quite a lot  although it's still  relatively complex  and it appears to me quite probable that  the home office is expediting the easy  later cases in order to  up the numbers that they can say  have been processed the latest figures  show 86 000 visas have been granted dina  koroshkos is one of them but only after  a second application was submitted and  approved in just six days she'll be  moving in with the bank's family and  western super mario there's hundreds if  not thousands of people in the same  position that we're in  who didn't put in that second  application like we did  and that continued to wait i think  dina is finally due to arrive in mid-may  the government says it's aware some  people have been waiting more than a  month to find out if their application  has been approved describing that as  unacceptable but they say that thousands  of visas are being processed every day  and work is being done to speed up the  system even further  for now marta and ilona must share a bed  in a bomb shelter  there's the safety of a home far from  the missiles in england but no one knows  when they'll finally get there  emma birchley sky news
 

